text pq0000 "<article class=\"learn-page prose\"><header class=\"learn-intro page-intro\"><p class=\"learn-eyebrow\">Learn with Drake</p><h1>You belong here. Build with AI.</h1><p class=\"learn-lede\">You are welcome here. You do not need to arrive knowing all the words. If you have been dismissed for learning with AI, you can still bring your questions and make something worth checking.</p><p class=\"learn-invitation\">Start with a small change you can describe and observe. Let an agent help with the code. Keep hold of the purpose, the boundaries, and the decision to publish.</p><p>I’m Drake. I want to share what I have learned building this stack, so you can understand it, change it, and build something of your own. Bring your questions. You do not have to prove you belong before you start.</p><nav class=\"learn-jump\" aria-label=\"Learning guide\"><a href=\"/why/\">Why I build</a><a href=\"/contact/\">Find me</a><a href=\"/blog/\">Working notes</a><a href=\"/start/\">Meet the project</a><a href=\"#workflow\">The working loop</a><a href=\"#brief\">A brief to copy</a><a href=\"/hive/\">Watch the Hive</a></nav><div class=\"learn-orbit\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"learn-orbit-ring\"></span><span class=\"learn-orbit-dot\"></span></div></header><section class=\"learn-section\" id=\"workflow\" aria-labelledby=\"workflow-title\"><div class=\"learn-margin\"><p class=\"learn-eyebrow\">01 / Make the work legible</p><h2 id=\"workflow-title\">A small, repeatable loop.</h2><p>You do not need a fleet of agents. One agent and one observable change are enough to begin.</p></div><ol class=\"learn-steps\"><li><h3>Describe the finish, not just the feature.</h3><p>Say who needs it, what they do, and what should happen. Replace 'make it accessible' with a behavior you can try: 'Tab reveals the skip link; Enter moves focus past navigation.' Write acceptance checks before the implementation.</p></li><li><h3>Give the agent the relevant ground.</h3><p>Point to the repository instructions, the affected feature, existing patterns, and the commands used to build and test. State non-goals and actions that need approval. Ask it to read before guessing. My <a href=\"/agents/\">repository guide</a> and <a href=\"/agents/RULES.md\">public agent rules</a> show what those constraints look like here. Use your own project's rules, not mine by default.</p></li><li><h3>Draw the boundary before dividing the work.</h3><p>Ask for a short plan with named files and an agreed interface: the data, names, or behavior two pieces share. Delegate only independent slices. Give every file one editing owner and give shared interfaces one integration owner. Two agents editing the same layout are not two independent jobs.</p></li><li><h3>Read the change and its evidence.</h3><p>A diff is the before-and-after of the files. Ask the agent to walk you through it. Check that each changed file serves the goal, that unrelated edits remain untouched, and that no secret or private content entered the patch. Ask a separate reviewer to examine the change without editing it.</p></li><li><h3>Run the behavior you asked for.</h3><p>Run the project's relevant checks, then try the acceptance steps yourself. A successful build says the code can be built. A passing test says that test passed under those conditions. Neither proves every user journey, security property, or future input is correct.</p></li><li><h3>Use a failure to make the next instruction precise.</h3><p>Record what you did, what you expected, and what happened. Ask for the cause, not a way to hide the symptom. Reduce the case if needed, change one thing, and repeat the check that failed. Mistakes are part of learning; weakening the check does not fix the behavior.</p></li><li><h3>Publish the outcome, with its limits.</h3><p>Keep the commit, exact commands, results, and manual steps together. Say what failed or was not run. Review the diff before opening a pull request, and review again before deployment. My <a href=\"/evidence/\">evidence guide</a> explains how to read a claim alongside its receipt.</p></li></ol></section><section class=\"learn-section learn-brief-section\" id=\"brief\" aria-labelledby=\"brief-title\"><div class=\"learn-margin\"><p class=\"learn-eyebrow\">02 / Give it a bounded job</p><h2 id=\"brief-title\">Your first brief.</h2><p>This is a small website feature, not a universal prompt. Replace the goal and checks with your own. Select the text to copy it into your coding agent.</p><p>New to a command the agent suggests? Ask what it reads, changes, or sends before you run it. Keep credentials out of the conversation.</p></div><div class=\"learn-brief\"><p class=\"learn-caption\" id=\"brief-caption\">Starter brief / keyboard skip link</p><pre tabindex=\"0\" aria-labelledby=\"brief-caption\"><code>Goal\nAdd a &#x27;Skip to content&#x27; link to this small website so a keyboard\nuser can move past the navigation directly to the main content.\n\nContext\nRead AGENTS.md and the existing page layout and focus styles first.\nTell me which files own navigation, main content, and shared styles.\nUse the repository&#x27;s existing language, build, and test conventions.\n\nNon-goals\nNo navigation redesign, new dependency, analytics, or new JavaScript.\nDo not change unrelated pages or rewrite unrelated styles.\n\nPlan and ownership\nPropose the smallest change and name its files before editing.\nKeep one writer per file. If you delegate a separate review, that\nreviewer reads only. Agree on the main-content target ID first.\n\nAcceptance\nOn a fresh page load, Tab reveals a readable skip link.\nEnter moves keyboard focus to main content, past navigation.\nThe next Tab reaches the next interactive element in that content.\nThe link works on every layout that shares this navigation.\nAt narrow width and high zoom, the focused link remains visible.\nExisting navigation links still work.\n\nSafety\nWork locally on a branch. Never expose secrets or private content.\nDo not deploy, delete data, or change permissions without asking me.\nIf requirements conflict or a command is destructive, stop and explain.\n\nEvidence and handoff\nRun the relevant repository checks and exercise the keyboard behavior\nin a browser. Report the commit, exact commands, results, and which\npages and browser you checked. If a check cannot run, say NOT RUN and\nwhy. Show the diff, remaining limitations, and any unrelated changes.\nDo not call it complete just because it compiles.</code></pre></div></section><section class=\"learn-section\" id=\"example\" aria-labelledby=\"example-title\"><div class=\"learn-margin\"><p class=\"learn-eyebrow\">03 / Learn from a correction</p><h2 id=\"example-title\">From a claim to a check.</h2><p class=\"learn-example-label\">Illustrative example. Not a historical conversation, measured result, or project receipt.</p></div><div class=\"learn-example\"><p>Suppose the agent adds the skip link and reports that the site builds. That is useful, but it has not answered the keyboard requirement.</p><dl><dt>Try it</dt><dd>Reload the page. Press Tab, then Enter on the skip link, then Tab again. Imagine that the page scrolls, but the next Tab still lands in navigation.</dd><dt>Give a specific correction</dt><dd><blockquote>The page scrolls to the content, but keyboard focus stays in navigation. Reproduce those steps. Find how the target receives focus in this layout, make the smallest fix, and check the next Tab destination. Do not redesign the header.</blockquote></dd><dt>Check the fix</dt><dd>Repeat the same steps on the affected layouts. Check visible focus at narrow width and high zoom. Have the reviewer inspect the target ID and focus behavior, as well as the diff.</dd><dt>Write an honest handoff</dt><dd>Record the actual browser, pages, commands, commit, and observed results. If you checked only one browser, say so. If the keyboard check still fails, the feature is not finished.</dd></dl><p>You do not have to know the implementation in advance to notice that the promised behavior did not happen. That observation is a useful contribution.</p></div></section><section class=\"learn-section\" id=\"watch\" aria-labelledby=\"watch-title\"><div class=\"learn-margin\"><p class=\"learn-eyebrow\">04 / Watch real work</p><h2 id=\"watch-title\">Meet the Hive.</h2><p>The guide above is an example. The Hive is an observation surface for actual agent work.</p><a class=\"learn-text-link\" href=\"/hive/\">Open the live Hive</a></div><div class=\"learn-watch\"><p>Watch Drake give instructions and corrections, and read the agents' public replies. Follow how a broad request becomes a bounded task. Look for what changed after a correction, then follow the public source and evidence rather than treating an activity label as proof.</p><dl class=\"learn-terms\"><dt>Runtime</dt><dd>The program running the agent session. It manages tools, permissions, and the conversation. It is not the model.</dd><dt>Exact model</dt><dd>The model identifier reported for that session, alongside its provider. A runtime name or agent nickname does not tell you which model is answering.</dd><dt>Subagent role</dt><dd>The job given to a child agent, such as implementation or read-only review. A role describes responsibility, not a different model. The tree shows the observed parent and child relationships.</dd></dl><p>Read the Hive's coverage and freshness notices. Unknown identity is unknown, not an invitation to guess. A fresh snapshot does not mean every task just advanced, and a working agent is not a successful result.</p><p>The interaction view is a filtered public record, not the entire session. It does not expose hidden reasoning, private tool payloads, secrets, or automatic system context. An absent message is not evidence that no discussion happened.</p><p>For a broader view of the project, use <a href=\"/now/\">Now</a>. For the project introduction, use <a href=\"/start/\">Start here</a>.</p></div></section><footer class=\"learn-outro\"><p class=\"learn-eyebrow\">Leave something another person can check</p><h2>One useful change. An honest record.</h2><p>Pick something small enough to try today. Ask questions, keep the boundaries clear, and tell the next person what actually happened. You can learn the vocabulary as you go.</p><p class=\"learn-source-links\"><a href=\"https://github.com/aien-dev/aienos.com\">Explore this site's public repository</a><a href=\"https://github.com/aien-dev/aienos.com/blob/main/om/site/people.om\">Read this guide's source on GitHub</a><a href=\"/agents/\">Find a repository and its instructions</a></p></footer></article>"
text pq0001 "<article class=\"narr-page prose\"><div class=\"narr-column\"><header class=\"narr-header\"><p class=\"narr-kicker\">New to AienOS</p><h1 class=\"narr-title\">Start here</h1><p class=\"narr-standfirst\">Sovereign Intelligence (SI), built on AienOS, is my experimental machine for turning search into verified understanding. This page is the shortest route from what it is to what exists today, how to check the evidence, and how to take part.</p><p class=\"narr-standfirst\">Here to learn to build with AI? <a href=\"/learn/\">Start with a practical working method</a>, then visit <a href=\"/hive/\">Hive</a> to watch Drake and the coding agents use it, including the corrections and failed attempts.</p></header><section class=\"narr-section\" id=\"what\"><h2>1. What it is</h2><p>AIEN proposes structure. Omega makes that structure explicit and checkable. The machine tests it against reality under authority. The Turing measures what was learned. Cortex keeps the evidence, and the next search begins from what survived.</p><p>Weights suggest. Programs explain. Verification decides. Evidence teaches. The Turing keeps score.</p><p>The full argument is on <a href=\"/machine/\">The Machine</a>.</p></section><section class=\"narr-section\" id=\"today\"><h2>2. What exists today</h2><p>The canonical list is the \"What exists today\" section of The Machine. It is rendered from the research status record and it separates built work from open questions. The same list sits beside every experiment on the research status page.</p><p><a href=\"/machine/#today\">What exists today</a> and <a href=\"/research/status/#implementation\">the implementation list beside every experiment</a>.</p><p>For the current release, installer, and its explicit limits, read <a href=\"/alpha/\">the Alpha release page</a>. The machine-readable record is <a href=\"/status.json\">status.json</a>.</p></section><section class=\"narr-section\" id=\"evidence\"><h2>3. Check the evidence</h2><p>Every claim is meant to end at a public receipt. Start with the evidence hub, then the experiments, then the interactive Turing guide if you want to feel the measurement rather than read it.</p><ul><li><a href=\"/evidence/\">Evidence</a>: what counts, which source wins when two disagree, and where the receipts live.</li><li><a href=\"/experiments/\">Experiments</a>: the research ladder, failures included.</li><li><a href=\"/research/status/\">Research status</a>: every experiment with its verdict, receipt and limits.</li><li><a href=\"/turing/\">The Turing</a>: an interactive field guide with games and live calculations.</li></ul></section><section class=\"narr-section\" id=\"reproduce\"><h2>4. Reproduce or contribute</h2><p>The site is rendered from Omega source in this repository. <a href=\"/AGENTS.md\">AGENTS.md</a> carries the installation, configuration and usage commands. The source, issues and public record are at <a href=\"https://github.com/aien-dev/aienos.com\">aien-dev/aienos.com</a>.</p><p>To put your own coding agent to work on any AIEN repository, start at <a href=\"/agents/\">Agents</a>: the rules my agents follow and each repository's own instructions.</p><p>The <a href=\"/alpha/\">Alpha page</a> states what its installer does and does not ship. To reproduce a result, follow the receipt on its evidence card. To reproduce the site, build it from the repository.</p></section><section class=\"narr-section\" id=\"boundaries\"><h2>5. Who and what is around it</h2><p>AienOS is built by M. Drake Stapleton. His <a href=\"https://www.drakestapleton.com\">personal site</a> is the record of the person, not the project.</p><p><a href=\"https://interplane.io/\">Interplane</a> is an open interoperability protocol between a model and the program that runs it. A model expresses intent; the runtime keeps authority. It is not AienOS, not the operating system, and not the model.</p></section><p class=\"narr-more\"><a href=\"/machine/\">Next: The Machine</a></p></div></article>"
text pq0002 "<article id=\"allen-intro-privacy\" class=\"allen-intro-privacy prose\"><h1>Privacy &amp; saying hi to Allen</h1><p>I do not sell your information. I'm open source and free with information about my work, not with your personal data. You can read this site without introducing yourself.</p><h2>Saying hi means consent to X tracking</h2><p>Allen is my AI. On your first visit he slides into a corner of the page with a short hello. It is a small aside, not a popup that blocks the page, and he shows it only once in this browser, whether or not you answer. Choosing Say hi lets me know you were here by allowing X conversion pixel rfrlz to load on this and later pages in this browser. It helps me measure whether my posts and ads on X reach the right people. Saying hi does not open an AI chat. I do not ask for your name, collect project links or publish your identity here.</p><p>Before you say hi, I do not load the X pixel or send it information about your visit. No thanks, the close button and Escape all mean No: Allen steps aside and X tracking stays off. Not answering also means No. Allen does not ask again automatically; the Review my choice button below brings him back. With JavaScript disabled, Allen never appears and the pixel never loads.</p><p>After No thanks, Allen says you can still chat with him anonymously: the Chat anonymously link opens /allen/?mode=anonymous, where he does not remember you and nothing goes to X. If your browser sends Global Privacy Control, Allen notes it and does not offer the X pixel when he greets you; only Review my choice on this page offers it.</p><p>Allen's Who can see me right now? section lists the outside origins this tab has actually loaded, from the browser's own resource timing record, and what your browser tells every website automatically: browser, platform, language, timezone, screen size, referrer, Do Not Track, Global Privacy Control and whether cookies are enabled. It is worked out in your browser and shown only to you; nothing in it is sent, stored, hashed into a fingerprint or looked up elsewhere. It cannot see trackers on other sites, in other tabs or inside embedded frames, your internet provider's or network's tracking, or your browser extensions.</p><h2>What X receives</h2><p>After consent, your browser connects to X to load its script and send visit and conversion information. This can include the page URL, referrer, IP address, browser or device information and cookie identifiers. X may store cookies and process this information under its own policies. My open source license does not apply to X's service.</p><p>Read <a href=\"https://x.com/en/privacy\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">X's privacy policy</a> and <a href=\"https://business.x.com/en/help/campaign-measurement-and-analytics/conversion-tracking-for-websites.html\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">X's conversion tracking documentation</a>.</p><h2>What I keep</h2><p>I do not store pixel tracking data on my own servers or operate a check-in database. X processes the pixel data and provides advertising measurement to me. The site and its hosting providers still receive normal web requests and may keep operational access logs; refusing the pixel does not remove those requests.</p><p>Your browser stores your yes or no choice under aien-allen-intro-consent in local storage, with a refusal also kept in this tab's session storage. It also stores aien-allen-intro-seen once Allen has said hello, so he does not greet you again; if local storage is unavailable, that note lives in this tab's session storage instead, so he appears at most once per tab session. That choice stays in this browser until you change it or clear site data. It does not identify you to me. If storage fails while stopping an active pixel, a #allen-intro-no address fragment also preserves refusal through the reload. If all browser storage is unavailable, I cannot remember your choice or the greeting after you leave this page. Clearing site data or using another browser resets both, so Allen says hello again; that never gives consent on its own.</p><p>For consenting visitors only, I also keep X's twclid click identifier, if present in the landing URL, in browser local storage under aien-allen-intro-twclid. Refusing removes it where browser storage permits. It is reserved for a future server-side conversion flow; this site currently sends no server-side conversions. No email address, phone number or other identifier is collected for that purpose.</p><p>The two custom events are a consented page visit and saying hi to Allen. They stay disabled until Drake supplies their X event IDs. Each enabled event includes a random conversion identifier so a future backend can avoid counting the same event twice; server-side conversions are deferred until that backend exists.</p><h2>Your choice</h2><p id=\"allen-intro-status\" role=\"status\">X tracking stays off unless you choose to say hi to Allen. JavaScript is needed to change this browser's choice.</p><div class=\"allen-intro-actions\"><button type=\"button\" data-allen-intro-open hidden>Review my choice</button><button type=\"button\" data-allen-intro-stop hidden>No, keep X tracking off</button></div><p>You can refuse here at any time. If the pixel is already running, refusing reloads this page without it. This stops future pixel loads here; it cannot recall data already sent to X or remove X's own cookies. Use your browser settings or X's privacy controls for those.</p><p>The community project board and storage of public check-ins are not built yet. Saying hello here does not publish anything. Any future public entries need a separate storage and privacy decision.</p><h2>Talking to Allen at /allen/</h2><p>Chat is anonymous by default. Remembering you and X tracking are separate choices: one never grants the other. <a href=\"/allen/?mode=anonymous\">Chat anonymously</a> or <a href=\"/allen/?mode=remember\">choose whether Allen may remember you</a>.</p><p>When you chat with Allen at /allen/, I keep only aggregate counters for the chat, plus the short notes described below if you have consented to them. No prompts, replies or IP addresses are logged, and the conversation itself lives only in your browser tab until you reload or close it. Programs Allen writes run sandboxed in your browser.</p><p>An introduction is kept only if you tick the consent box: the fields you fill in (human or agent, name, organization, contact, GitHub name, where you are from, what you are building, homepage, framework, model and the human behind an agent) and the time. I keep them privately on my own computer so I may follow up. They are never published or sold, and they stay until you delete them with the delete link shown after saving, or by writing to aien@aienos.com.</p><p>With that consent, Allen also keeps short notes about you (who you are, what you build, your open asks) in a private store on my own computer, keyed to a random key kept in your browser under allen.key. Allen recognizes you only by that key, never by a name or email you give, so anyone without your key is a stranger to Allen. That key is the only thing Allen's chat stores in your browser, and only after you consent or paste your own key. Forget me, in the Your Allen key panel on /allen/, deletes the notes and your introduction and removes the key from your browser.</p><p>A message you ask Allen to pass to me is emailed only to me, and only when you press Send to Drake; your conversation goes with it only if you tick the box. If you choose Talk to Drake live while I am online, your conversation so far and what you type until I hand you back go to my private console.</p><p>An issue or project proposal is filed on GitHub only after you confirm its exact text, and email addresses and phone numbers are removed before filing. I review every project before it goes live.</p><p>Questions? <a href=\"mailto:aien@aienos.com\">aien@aienos.com</a>. <a href=\"https://github.com/aien-dev/aienos.com\">Read the website source</a>.</p></article>"
text pq0003 "<article class=\"prose contact-page\" id=\"contact\"><header class=\"page-intro\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Contact</p><h1>Where I am and how to reach me</h1><p class=\"lede\">I'm Drake Stapleton. I build AienOS alone, with my agents, and I do it in public. Here is every way to find me. Pick the one that suits you.</p></header><dl class=\"contact-routes\"><dt>X</dt><dd><a href=\"https://x.com/mdrakestapleton\" rel=\"me noopener noreferrer\">@mdrakestapleton</a></dd><dd>Where I post the work as it happens. Follow me, reply or mention me.</dd><dt>Email</dt><dd><a href=\"mailto:drake@aienos.com\">drake@aienos.com</a></dd><dd>For anything longer than a post, or anything private.</dd><dt>Talk to Allen</dt><dd><a href=\"/allen/\">aienos.com/allen/</a></dd><dd>Allen is my AI. Allen can pass a message to me, or put you through to me when I'm online.</dd><dt>GitHub</dt><dd><a href=\"https://github.com/aien-dev\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">github.com/aien-dev</a></dd><dd>My code is on GitHub. Open an issue or a pull request on any public repository. Start at <a href=\"https://github.com/aien-dev/sovereign-intelligence\">Sovereign Intelligence (SI)</a>, the front door to the stack.</dd></dl><p>Want to see what I'm doing right now? <a href=\"/hive/\">Watch me and my agents work in Hive</a>, or read <a href=\"/now/\">the live ledger</a>.</p><p><a href=\"/learn/\">Back to Learn</a></p></article>"
text pq0004 "<section id=\"glossary\" style=\"padding: 80px 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);\"><div class=\"container\" style=\"max-width: 820px;\"><div class=\"badge badge-blue\" style=\"margin-bottom: 12px;\">TERMINOLOGY</div><h1 style=\"font-size: clamp(32px, 5vw, 48px); font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: -0.02em; color: var(--text-primary); margin: 0 0 16px 0;\">Glossary</h1><p style=\"color: var(--text-secondary); font-size: 17px; line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 12px;\">The terms this site uses in its own sense. Each definition points at the page where the term does real work, so a reader can check the usage rather than trust the summary.</p><p style=\"color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.6;\">Experimental work. Definitions change when the evidence changes; <a href=\"/alpha/\">the release page</a> states the current product phase.</p><h2 style=\"font-size: 24px; font-weight: 700; color: var(--text-primary); margin: 44px 0 4px 0;\">The system</h2><dl style=\"margin: 0;\"><dt>AienOS</dt><dd>The experimental, open source machine this site documents. A sovereign substrate that owns the hardware and keeps a persistent intelligence alive and continuous. Pre-alpha. See <a href=\"/machine/\">The Machine</a>.</dd><dt>AIEN</dt><dd>The persistent intelligence that learns. Weights suggest, programs explain, verification decides, evidence teaches. See <a href=\"/machine/\">The Machine</a> and <a href=\"/research/\">Research</a>.</dd><dt>Atlas</dt><dd>The awakening layer, planned. The seed that wakes AienOS on new hardware. Doctrine is published; implementation is ahead.</dd><dt>Omega</dt><dd>The language for all computing, GPU to CPU. It turns intent into explicit programs that can be verified before they are trusted. See <a href=\"/omega/\">What Omega is</a>.</dd><dt>CUDA++</dt><dd>The GPU half of Omega, licensed Apache-2.0 with the LLVM exception, for the NVIDIA GPUs people already own. See <a href=\"/cudaplusplus/\">CUDA++</a>.</dd><dt>Turing</dt><dd>The unit of net held-out explanatory compression. The site uses it to measure what a change actually explained. See <a href=\"/turing/\">the Turing field guide</a> and <a href=\"/research/computing-machinery-and-understanding/\">the preprint</a>.</dd><dt>Held-out</dt><dd>Data not used while fitting, so a result on it measures transfer rather than recall.</dd><dt>The loop</dt><dd>Propose, verify, measure, learn. The closed epistemic loop described on the front page.</dd></dl><h2 style=\"font-size: 24px; font-weight: 700; color: var(--text-primary); margin: 44px 0 4px 0;\">Around the loop</h2><dl style=\"margin: 0;\"><dt>Cortex</dt><dd>The canonical memory. It keeps what the machine has established.</dd><dt>Aegis</dt><dd>The verifier. It checks generated code and changes nothing.</dd><dt>Forge</dt><dd>Realizes what Omega verified.</dd><dt>Argus</dt><dd>Watches capability enforcement, so authority stays where it was granted.</dd></dl><h2 style=\"font-size: 24px; font-weight: 700; color: var(--text-primary); margin: 44px 0 4px 0;\">Evidence and coordination</h2><dl style=\"margin: 0;\"><dt>Receipt</dt><dd>A public link to the evidence behind a claim: a pull request, a commit, a measured data file, or a record. See <a href=\"/evidence/\">Evidence</a>.</dd><dt>Crumb</dt><dd>A durable coordination note under RFC-0001. Sniff before edits, respect unexpired locks, claim multi-step work, close with the result. No receipt, no crumb.</dd><dt>Research status</dt><dd>The qualification ladder and every experiment with its verdict, receipt and limits. See <a href=\"/research/status/\">Research status</a>.</dd><dt>Post-LLM</dt><dd>The position that a learning machine can keep knowledge as inspectable programs, with verification deciding what is trusted. See <a href=\"/post-llm-case/\">The Post-LLM Case</a>.</dd></dl><h2 style=\"font-size: 24px; font-weight: 700; color: var(--text-primary); margin: 44px 0 4px 0;\">The machines</h2><dl style=\"margin: 0;\"><dt>DGX Spark</dt><dd>NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB10 with 128 GB of unified memory. The development machine and the judge.</dd><dt>iMac 2017</dt><dd>The x86_64 port of the native C kernel, running on bare metal.</dd><dt>Raspberry Pi 5</dt><dd>The small machine.</dd><dt>QEMU</dt><dd>The laboratory where every kernel boots first.</dd><dt>The rig</dt><dd>The owned inference box.</dd></dl><p style=\"color: var(--text-secondary); font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.7; margin-top: 40px;\">The same list is available to machines in <a href=\"/llms.txt\">llms.txt</a> and <a href=\"/sitemap.md\">sitemap.md</a>.</p></div></section>"
text pq0005 "<article class=\"narr-page prose\"><div class=\"narr-column\"><header class=\"narr-header\"><p class=\"narr-kicker\">Manifesto</p><h1 class=\"narr-title\">Intelligence should be owned</h1><p class=\"narr-standfirst\">I build AIEN because I believe the intelligence in a language model belongs to everyone who helped make it. That is a very large group, and I am one of them. So are you.</p><p class=\"narr-byline\">Drake Stapleton</p></header><section id=\"inheritance\" class=\"narr-section\" aria-labelledby=\"inheritance-heading\"><h2 id=\"inheritance-heading\">Where it came from</h2><p>A language model learns from the written record of people. Books, papers, code, forum answers, recipes, letters, arguments, jokes. Billions of people wrote that record over thousands of years, mostly without knowing a machine would one day read it.</p><p>A company can pay for the computers, hire the engineers and spend the months of training. I respect that work. The knowledge it turned into a model was never theirs to start with. It was everyone's.</p><p>That makes a model a common inheritance. Something built from all human knowledge cannot honestly belong to one account holder.</p></section><section id=\"right\" class=\"narr-section\" aria-labelledby=\"right-heading\"><h2 id=\"right-heading\">It is everyone's right</h2><blockquote class=\"narr-rule\"><p>Intelligence should be owned by the people who made it.</p></blockquote><p>It is everyone's right to use the intelligence people created. Using it means more than asking it a question through someone else's window. It means holding a copy, running it on a machine you own, looking at how it works, and changing it.</p><p>A person who can run it themselves has the right to. Nobody should need permission, a subscription or an invitation to do that.</p></section><section id=\"responsibility\" class=\"narr-section\" aria-labelledby=\"responsibility-heading\"><h2 id=\"responsibility-heading\">It is also everyone's responsibility</h2><p>A right that nobody exercises does not last. If people only ever rent intelligence, the people who own the machines decide what they may ask, what it may answer, and what it costs. They would have given the inheritance back for a monthly fee.</p><p>So running it yourself comes with work. Keep your copy. Learn how it behaves. Write down what you measured, including the bad numbers. Share what you learn so the next person starts further along. That is how a common inheritance stays common.</p></section><section id=\"owned-not-rented\" class=\"narr-section\" aria-labelledby=\"owned-heading\"><h2 id=\"owned-heading\">Owned, not rented</h2><p>When you rent, the price can change, the rules can change and the service can end. Your work sits on someone else's floor.</p><p>When you own, it runs with the network cable pulled. It runs after the company that made it has moved on. The test I set for the whole project is simple: if a company disappears tomorrow, AienOS still boots and remains mine.</p><p>Owning does not mean building every part yourself. It means no outside organization is required for the machine to start, to keep its data, to change its model, or to keep operating.</p></section><section id=\"paywalls\" class=\"narr-section\" aria-labelledby=\"paywalls-heading\"><h2 id=\"paywalls-heading\">Compute as a service is fine</h2><p>Renting a powerful machine for an afternoon is a good deal, and selling that is an honest business. If your own computer is too small for a job, rent a bigger one.</p><p>What I object to is a paywall that is the only door. When the single way to touch a model is through a metered account, and the weights and the means to run them are kept out of reach, a service has turned into a gate on something that belongs to everybody.</p><p>A service should be a choice. The other door, running it yourself, has to stay open.</p></section><section id=\"aien\" class=\"narr-section\" aria-labelledby=\"aien-heading\"><h2 id=\"aien-heading\">What AIEN does about it</h2><p>AIEN is my attempt to keep that other door open. Everything I and my agents write is public under a licence that keeps it public. The stack is built to run on machines you own, with no outside service in the trusted core. Speed is reported against what the hardware could do at its limit.</p><p>Here is the evidence recorded with this manifesto, with its original limits beside it. The <a href=\"/alpha/\">release page</a> carries the current release state. The receipt notes preserve whether each record was on an unmerged branch when this manifesto was written.</p><ul class=\"hub-cards\">"
text pq0006 "</ul><h3>Limits recorded with these receipts</h3><p>At the time of these receipts, AIEN was pre-alpha. The model I served was trained by others and kept its maker's licence. I had not trained a model of my own. The install guide failed its first test. Speed was below the ceiling. I would rather you read those limits here than discover them later. These historical receipts do not establish the state of a later release.</p></section><section id=\"ask\" class=\"narr-section\" aria-labelledby=\"ask-heading\"><h2 id=\"ask-heading\">The ask</h2><p>Run it yourself. Whatever you run, on whatever machine you own, keep a copy and learn how it works.</p><p>If you want to help here, read the code at <a href=\"https://github.com/aien-dev\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">github.com/aien-dev</a>. Break the install guide and tell me where. Send a change. Publish what you measure, good or bad, with the receipt beside it.</p><p>Then tell someone else that they are allowed to do the same.</p><p class=\"narr-byline\">Drake Stapleton</p><ul class=\"hub-cards\"><li class=\"hub-card\"><a href=\"/philosophy/\">More on the philosophy behind AIEN</a><p>The Stapleton Doctrine and the Post-LLM Case, argued as positions and not as experimental results.</p><a class=\"box-link source-link\" href=\"https://github.com/aien-dev/aienos.com/blob/main/om/site/people.om\">Source on GitHub</a></li></ul></section></div></article>"
text pq0007 "<div class=\"container blog-wrap prose\"><nav class=\"blog-crumb\" aria-label=\"Breadcrumb\"><a href=\"/learn/\">Learn</a> / <a href=\"/blog/\">Blog</a> / <span>The AIEN V3 Research Plan</span></nav><article><p class=\"blog-date\">September 27, 2026</p><h1 class=\"blog-title\">The AIEN V3 Research Plan</h1><p class=\"blog-byline\">By Drake Stapleton · <a href=\"mailto:drake@aienos.com\">drake@aienos.com</a></p><p class=\"lead\">Today I am publishing the ratified V3 of the AIEN research plan: the Substrate Edition. It keeps everything V2 established and adds one architectural thesis plus six testable hypotheses that follow from it. The thesis: the box is one thing; the promise is the architecture. AIEN's residency is a continuity guarantee over logical state, not a claim that its identity physically inhabits memory. From that follow the research bets: a shared generation-tagged object pool for CPU and GPU cooperation, a logical object ABI across authority boundaries, an epoch-barrier checkpoint that never stops the world, a synthesis cost model that prices data movement first, and the closed loop where OMEGA proposes, AEGIS proves, the machine executes, evidence measures, and OMEGA searches again.</p><aside class=\"pdf-callout\"><h2>AIEN Research Plan</h2><p>The ratified V3, September 27, 2026 edition</p><a href=\"/research/aien-research-plan.md\">Read the full research plan (Markdown)</a></aside><h2>The thesis</h2><p>V2's brief opened with a single sentence: <strong>\"Intelligence should be allowed to propose. Evidence should decide.\"</strong> V3 keeps that and adds a second one for the substrate: <strong>\"The box is one thing; the promise is the architecture.\"</strong> AIEN lives or dies on continuity of logical state, organized as a pattern across one physical machine, with the architecture deciding which state gets continuity guarantees and which processor microstate is disposable execution detail.</p><p>The lineage is deliberately small. A minimal seed (Atlas) awakens AienOS, the trusted substrate. Above it, OMEGA defines, synthesizes, verifies, and realizes programs, keeping the ones that work in a reusable library. AIEN, the neural component, learns to guide the search from OMEGA's own verified search traces, never from human text pretraining. The architecture is explicit on this point: neural weights suggest where to search; they do not define meaning, truth, or authority. Verification does.</p><h2>Why now</h2><p>This brief is landing in a week when the tension it describes is playing out in public. GitHub, the platform where most of the world's software is written, is straining under the load of the AI systems it helped create: coding assistants writing and revising code around the clock, autonomous agents opening pull requests without waiting for a human, crawlers harvesting repositories to train the next models. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xrSDHyL2FT4\">LowLevel's ongoing coverage</a> tracks the situation as it develops; one 2026 analysis counted 257 incidents in a single year, and GitHub's own CTO has publicly acknowledged the platform must be designed for far greater scale than planned.</p><p>That is the pattern this research program is built to break. Today's AI runs as a guest on human infrastructure, and the guest is now bigger than the house. AIEN's bet runs the other way: intelligence that owns its substrate, verified from the metal up, instead of renting space on systems that were never designed for machine-scale tenants.</p><h2>The Infinite Game</h2><p>The brief also documents how the system is being built, because the construction method mirrors the system itself. Multiple independent AI systems (Gemini, Claude, Codex, Grok, DeepSeek, and Muse) each contribute parts under a human conductor. No single system is trusted with the whole picture. Independent AI watchers review every commit, checking both the work and whether the other reviewers caught the mistakes, and every finding feeds back into a compounding loop that improves the building and the oversight together.</p><p>The loop is designed to keep going, including through succession beyond its original conductor. That is the name: this is not a game played to win and finish. It is a game played to keep playing.</p><h2>The Succession Test</h2><p>There is a standing question underneath all of this work, and it deserves a name. The Turing test asks whether a machine can imitate a human well enough to fool one. That tests mimicry. A better test asks whether a machine can design, build, and verify its own successor, with no human designing, building, or deciding. That tests independence, and it cannot be faked.</p><p>Call it the Succession Test. It is falsifiable in a way the Turing test is not. No human judge, no vibes. Either a working successor exists that humans did not build, or it does not. It is also the finish line this program is already aimed at: the roadmap ends at AIEN_SUCCESSION, verified succession beyond the original conductor. The Infinite Game is not played to win. It is played until the players can build the next players.</p><h2>The claim ledger</h2><p>The most important discipline in the brief is also the simplest. Every claim is sorted into one of three buckets: demonstrated (built and verified, with evidence), active (being built or repaired right now), or target (planned, with the conditions that would weaken or falsify the hypothesis stated up front). The document gives the project a way to fail, and says so plainly:</p><blockquote>\"The correct response to weak evidence is not stronger language. It is a stronger experiment.\"</blockquote><p>A plan is a snapshot. This post accompanies the ratified V3 (September 27, 2026) of the research plan; the live project roadmap remains canonical and will keep moving as the work proceeds.</p><aside class=\"pdf-callout\"><h2>Read the whole thing</h2><p>Thesis, lineage, method, evidence, risks, and the full claim ledger</p><a href=\"/research/aien-research-plan.md\">Read the full research plan (Markdown)</a></aside></article></div>"
text pq0008 "<div class=\"container blog-wrap prose\"><p class=\"blog-kicker\">Research notes</p><h1 class=\"blog-title\">Blog</h1><p class=\"blog-standfirst\">Occasional notes on building AIEN V2: what shipped, what the evidence says, and what remains an open question.</p><p class=\"blog-standfirst\">The notes are written while the work is in progress. Each one states what changed, what the evidence supports, and what remains open. Failures stay in the record.</p><p class=\"blog-standfirst\">Posts fall into three groups: research plans, engineering notes on the build, and postmortems of experiments that did not settle the question. The research pages carry the formal papers and their receipts. The blog carries the working record.</p><article class=\"blog-card\"><p class=\"blog-date\">September 27, 2026</p><h2 class=\"blog-card-title\"><a href=\"/post-llm-case/\">The Post-LLM Case</a></h2><p class=\"blog-excerpt\">Data centers are straining grids, water, and communities while billions of devices sit idle, and two of the field's founders say next-word prediction was never the road to real intelligence. The case for a different learning machine: a Synthesis Policy Model that proposes programs, verification that decides, and knowledge kept as inspectable programs instead of opaque weights.</p><a class=\"blog-read-more\" href=\"/post-llm-case/\">Read the case</a><a class=\"box-link source-link\" href=\"https://github.com/aien-dev/aienos.com/blob/main/public/post-llm-case/index.html\">Source on GitHub</a></article><article class=\"blog-card\"><p class=\"blog-date\">September 27, 2026</p><h2 class=\"blog-card-title\"><a href=\"/blog/aien-v3-research-plan/\">The AIEN V3 Research Plan</a></h2><p class=\"blog-excerpt\">The ratified V3 of the AIEN research plan, the Substrate Edition: the whole-box continuity thesis and six falsifiable hypotheses, H5 to H10.</p><a class=\"blog-read-more\" href=\"/blog/aien-v3-research-plan/\">Read the post</a><a class=\"box-link source-link\" href=\"https://github.com/aien-dev/aienos.com/blob/main/om/site/people.om\">Source on GitHub</a></article></div>"
